@startuml
' REVIEWED_MAIN_2026_05_13: aligned to current merged code; large-investment retained pending simplification merge.
title WYSIWYS for Corda Protocol V1 — Tier 1 Critical Operations\n(Corrected: What Each Layer Actually Verifies)
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam responseMessageBelowArrow true
skinparam wrapWidth 460
autonumber

box "Environment A (Assumed Untrusted)" #LightPink
actor "Dev / Manager\n(Initiator)" as Init
participant "Solana CLI / UI\n(Untrusted)" as UI
end box

box "Communication" #White
participant "Slack/PagerDuty\n(OOB Channel)" as OOB
end box

box "Environment B (OOB Verification — Software Trust)" #LightYellow
actor "Tier 1 Signer\n(4/7 Cold)" as Appr
participant "OOB Multisig Client\n(e.g., Squads App)" as App
participant "Clean Device\n(Solana CLI)" as Clean
end box

box "Environment C (Zero-Trust HW)" #LightGreen
participant "Advanced HW\n(Keystone/GridPlus)" as HW
end box

box "Infrastructure" #LightBlue
participant "Private RPC" as RPC
participant "CI/CD Repo\n(Deterministic Build)" as GitHub
database "Solana Mainnet\n(Squads V4)" as Squads
end box

== Phase 1: On-Chain Proposal ==
note over Init, Appr
**Note:** In Tier 1 multisig (4-of-7 cold), the Initiator
and each Approver are always **different persons**.
This separation is mandatory for critical operations.
end note

Init -> UI: Draft Critical Action\n(Program Upgrade or Config Change)
UI -> Squads: `vault_transaction_create`\n& `vault_transaction_add_instruction`\n(inner IX stored in Proposal PDA)
Squads --> UI: Returns Proposal Index #12

note over UI #MistyRose
**RISK: Compromised UI / CLI**
If the Solana CLI or Web UI is malicious, the inner
instructions written to the Proposal PDA may differ
from what the Initiator intended. The on-chain payload
is the source of truth, not the UI display.
For program upgrades: a compromised CLI could point
the BPF loader to a malicious buffer address.
end note

== Phase 2: Out-of-Band Hand-off ==
Init -> OOB: "Tier 1: Please approve pending proposal.\nIntent: Upgrade vault program to build [e7b4...f9a1]"
OOB -> Appr: Receives notification (serves only as an alert)

note over OOB #MistyRose
**RISK: Social Engineering**
OOB message is human-authored free text.
A compromised Initiator could state a legitimate
build hash for a malicious on-chain proposal that
points to a different buffer address entirely.
end note

== Phase 3: Intent Verification via Polling & Squads App ==
note over App #PaleGreen
**MITIGATION 1: HARDCODED CONFIG POLLING**
The OOB Multisig Client ignores user input for addresses
and auto-polls the Solana RPC for the **hardcoded multisig PDA**,
entirely defeating address poisoning and UI spoofing.

**MITIGATION 2: PRIMARY INTENT VERIFICATION**
The Client operates **out-of-band** from the untrusted UI/CLI.
It decodes the fetched **inner instructions** from the active
proposal to display a true, human-readable state change.
end note

App -> RPC: Poll active Proposal PDAs\nfor hardcoded Multisig address
RPC --> App: Returns active proposals & VaultTransaction inner IX bytes

alt Option A: Program Upgrade (BPF Loader)
    App -> Appr: Shows pending Proposal #12:\n**"Upgrade Program [ProgID]"**\n**"Using Buffer: [BufferAddr]"**
else Option B: Critical Config Change
    App -> Appr: Shows pending Proposal #12:\n**"Config Change"**\n**"Update Curator Authority"**\n**"New Key: [Pubkey_Address]"**
end

note over App #MistyRose
**RISK: Squads App shows WHAT, not WHY**
For config changes, the Squads App decode is sufficient
(new key, new threshold — the intent is self-evident).
For program upgrades, the App shows the buffer address
but **cannot verify what code is inside that buffer**.
The approver needs an additional step to verify
the buffer contents match the audited build.
end note

== Phase 4: Buffer Hash Verification (Program Upgrades Only) ==
note over Clean #PaleGreen
**MITIGATION: BUFFER CONTENT VERIFICATION**
A clean device (separate from both the untrusted UI
and the Squads App) runs two standard Solana CLI
commands to independently verify the buffer contents.
No custom tooling required.
end note

Appr -> Clean: Reads buffer address from Squads App
Clean -> RPC: `$ solana program dump <BufferAddr>`\n`  /tmp/buffer.so --url mainnet-beta`
RPC --> Clean: Returns raw compiled bytecode
Clean -> Clean: `$ sha256sum /tmp/buffer.so`\n→ **"e7b4...f9a1"**
Clean -> GitHub: Compare hash against CI/CD build
GitHub --> Clean: Expected Hash: **"e7b4...f9a1"**

alt Hashes Match
    Clean -> Appr: **"✓ HASH VERIFIED: [e7b4...f9a1]"**
else Hashes Do NOT Match
    Clean -> Appr: **"⚠️ HASH MISMATCH"**\n**"Expected: [e7b4...f9a1]"**\n**"Got: [a3c1...d8f2]"**\n**"DO NOT SIGN"**
end

note over Clean, GitHub #MistyRose
**RISK: Compromised Clean Device or RPC**
If the clean device or RPC returns fake bytecode,
the hash comparison will be fooled.
**Mitigation:** Use a different RPC than Environment A.
**Mitigation:** Cross-check buffer on a block explorer
(Solscan, SolanaFM) from yet another device.
**Mitigation:** 4-of-7 threshold requires fooling
four signers' clean devices independently.
end note

== Phase 5: Hardware Wallet — Program & Account Verification ==
note over App, HW
**IMPORTANT: What the HW wallet actually signs**
The HW wallet signs a `proposal_approve` instruction
to the Squads program — NOT the inner BPF upgrade IX.
The inner IX (buffer address, program ID, upgrade hash)
lives in the Proposal PDA and is **not part of the
payload** sent to the HW wallet.
end note

App -> HW: Pass `proposal_approve` TX\nvia QR Code / USB

note right of HW
**HW SCREEN (with Squads firmware support):**
Program: Squads V4 (SQDS...4nkn)
Action:  proposal_approve
Multisig: Bk4R...mN21
Member:  9xK2...4cD7 (signer)

**HW SCREEN (without Squads firmware support):**
Program: SMPLec...4nkn
Data:    0x03 0C 00 00 ...
Acct[0]: 9xK2...4cD7 (signer, writable)
⚠️ Unrecognized instruction
end note

note over HW #MistyRose
**RISK: No Inner Intent on HW Screen**
The hardware wallet **NEVER** displays
"Upgrade vault to hash [e7b4...f9a1]".
It can only show the Squads wrapper instruction.
The HW provides:
✓ Correct program ID (Squads, not a phishing contract)
✓ Correct multisig PDA address
✓ Signer key identity
✓ Private key protection (secure element)
✗ Inner BPF upgrade details (buffer addr, program hash)
✗ Inner config change details (new authority key)
end note

HW -> Appr: HW Screen displays:\n**Squads: proposal_approve**\nMultisig: Bk4R...mN21\nMember: 9xK2...4cD7

== Phase 6: Final Intent Check (Three Independent Sources) ==
note over Appr
**FINAL INTENT CHECK**
Approver verifies consistency across three independent sources:

**For Program Upgrade:**
  1. **Squads App (Phase 3):** "Upgrade [ProgID] using buffer [BufferAddr]"
  2. **Clean Device (Phase 4):** "HASH VERIFIED: [e7b4...f9a1]" ✓
  3. **HW Screen (Phase 5):** Correct Squads program + multisig ✓

**For Config Change:**
  1. **Squads App (Phase 3):** "Update Curator to [Pubkey]" → matches OOB intent ✓
  2. **HW Screen (Phase 5):** Correct Squads program + multisig ✓
  (No buffer hash check needed — intent is self-evident from decode)

All three sources keyed off the same **Proposal #12**.
end note

note over Appr #MistyRose
**RESIDUAL RISK**
If the Squads App (Phase 3), the Clean Device (Phase 4),
AND the OOB channel (Phase 2) are all compromised,
the HW wallet alone cannot detect a malicious inner IX.
For Tier 1: 4-of-7 threshold means an attacker must
fool **four independent signers** across **three
independent verification layers each**.
end note

Appr -> HW: Physically approves on device
HW --> App: Returns Cryptographic Signature\n(signs only the `proposal_approve` TX)

== Phase 7: Broadcast & Timelock ==
App -> RPC: Broadcast signed `proposal_approve` TX
RPC -> Squads: Execute `ApproveTransaction`
Squads --> App: State: Approver added

note over Squads: **Tier 1 Logic:**\nIf 4/7 Threshold met:\nStatus = 'Approved'\n**72h Timelock SLA begins.**

note over Squads #PaleGreen
**MITIGATION: Timelock as Last Defence**
Even if all prior checks were fooled, the **72h timelock**
provides a final window to detect and **veto** the action.
Requires active monitoring of approved proposals
against expected actions (watchtower / alerting).
This is an **on-chain guarantee** — cannot be bypassed
without compromising the multisig threshold itself.
For program upgrades: independent hash verification
of the buffer can be re-run during the 72h window
by any team member on any device.
end note

App -> Appr: "Success. 72h Timelock Active."

@enduml